ou[sg.] are a good man.\par \par 10.\emdash  I am, you[sg.] are, he is, to be.\par }{\f208\lang1036 \par }{\f208 11.\emdash  I can, you[sg.] can, he can, to be able.\par \par 12.\emdash  My friend, your[sg.] family.\par \par 13.\emdash  for-great joy, [the] Italian language.\par \par }{\b\f210 NOTES:}{\f208 \par 1. }{\b\f210 Quid}{\f208  and not }{\b\f210 quis}{\f208 , as in }{\b\f210 Quis v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nit}{\f208  of L 2, P 1 (lesson no. 2, phrase 1) because it is about a thing and not a person.\par 2. }{\b\f210 P}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ssum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I can}{\f208 , is a verb composed with the verb }{\b\f210 sum}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I am}{\f208 . We see that verbs do not all have the same ending: compare, for example, with }{\b\f210 l}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 quor}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I speak}{\f208  (L 1) }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nio}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I come}{\f208  (L 2), }{\b\f210 do}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I give}{\f208  (L 2). But do not be frightened, you will see with use that the forms of the Latin verb are actually simpler than those of the French verb.\par 3. }{\b\f210 Ne}{\f208  is particle placed at the end of a word to indicate a question: }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 tes}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 you can}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 pot}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 sne}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 can you?}{\f208 \par 4. }{\b\f210 M}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 gno g}{\f208 au}{\b\f210 dio}{\f208 : as in French, the adjective agrees with the noun to which it relates; this agreement is often marked by similar endings, as in the couple of words that you will find in sentences 12 and 13, but this is not a general rule.\par 5. You have already seen }{\b\f210 Roma}{\f208  (L 1, P 6). Here, saying }{\b\f210 Romam}{\f208  instead of }{\b\f210 Roma}{\f208  makes it possible to specify that Rome is the }{\b\f210 place where (whither) one goes}{\f208 . Also note that }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 gno g}{\f208 au}{\b\f210 dio}{\f208  means something more than just }{\i\f211 great joy}{\f208 , which would actually be: }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 gnum g}{\f208 au}{\b\f210 dium}{\f208 . Little by little, you will get used to these differences. For the time being, do not worry about them!\par \par }{\i\f211 You should be content with learning words and phrases the way young children do. If you do make a mistake, it will be no more serious than when a young child says, "des chevals" instead of saying "}{\b\i\f212 des chevaux}{\i\f211 ". It is necessary to go through that!\par }{\f208 \par }{\b\f210 EXERCITATIO:}{\f208 \par \par 1.\emdash  What is your[sg.] friend seeking?\par 2.\emdash  My friend [is looking for] (}{\i\f211 seeks}{\f208 ) a letter.\par 3.\emdash  The postman can accept a lot of wine.\par }{\f208\lang1036 4}{\f208 .\emdash }{\f208\lang1036  }{\f208 The Italian language is not difficult.\par }{\f208\lang1036 5}{\f208 .\emdash }{\f208\lang1036  }{\f208 It gives him great pleasure (}{\i\f211 to-him}{\f208  }{\i\f211 for-great}{\f208  }{\i\f211 joy}{\f208  }{\i\f211 is}{\f208 ) to speak Latin.\par 6.\emdash  Can you[sg.] come home?\par 7.\emdash  Yes, Sir, I can come.\par \par }{\i\f211 Starting from the next lesson, you will find the final form of our lessons: on the left page will be the Latin text, and opposite this on the right page, the French equivalent as well as explanatory notes to which the numerals in brackets refer.}{\par }}